<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Belegezites</h1><p class="st-meta">600 CE – 900 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Slavic tribe settled in Thessaly in the 7th century, remembered for trading grain with besieged Thessalonica.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/berziti">Belegezites</a> were one of the Slavic groups that settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> during the great migrations of the late 6th and 7th centuries. They occupied lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaly">Thessaly</a> around Phthiotic <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/thebes">Thebes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demetrias">Demetrias</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pagasetic-gulf">Gulf of Volos</a>. Almost everything known about them comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/miracles-of-saint-demetrius">Miracles of Saint Demetrius</a>, a Byzantine collection of stories about <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>They lived as farming communities under their own chiefs, partly independent of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a>. During the Slavic siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thessaloniki">Thessalonica</a> in the 670s they did not join the attackers but sold grain to the starving city, a sign of their prosperity and pragmatic relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a>. Their name probably survives in Velegezitia, a district named in later Byzantine sources.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Over the 8th and 9th centuries <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/byzantium">Byzantium</a> reasserted control over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theme-system">theme system</a>, and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/berziti">Belegezites</a> were gradually brought under imperial administration, converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> and absorbed into the Greek-speaking population. They leave few traces apart from place names.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="chiefdom, 600 CE – 900 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><ellipse cx="44" cy="56" rx="16" ry="24"/><path d="M44 34 V78"/><path d="M68 20 V84"/><path d="M68 20 L63 30 H73 Z"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">chiefdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:71.64%;width:5.97%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>600 CE – 900 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Slavic paganism, later Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Velegezites</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=750&amp;focus=belegezites" aria-label="Belegezites on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5213 767 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Belegezites lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5533 1023h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M5533 1023h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Greece. <a href="/atlas?year=750&amp;focus=belegezites">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

